1
00:00:06,870 --> 00:00:09,500
Here's the problem with teleportation.

2
00:00:12,840 --> 00:00:14,700
Lay it on me.

3
00:00:14,870 --> 00:00:16,820
Assuming a device could be invented

4
00:00:16,830 --> 00:00:18,670
Which would identify the quantum state of matter

5
00:00:18,680 --> 00:00:20,410
Of an individual in one location

6
00:00:20,420 --> 00:00:23,950
And transmit that pattern to a distant location for reassembly,

7
00:00:23,950 --> 00:00:26,010
You would not have actually transported the individual.

8
00:00:26,020 --> 00:00:28,240
You would have destroyed him in one location

9
00:00:28,250 --> 00:00:30,660
And recreated him in another.

10
00:00:32,280 --> 00:00:34,050
How about that.

11
00:00:35,030 --> 00:00:36,910
Personally, I would never use a transporter,

12
00:00:36,920 --> 00:00:39,000
Because the original sheldon would have to be

13
00:00:39,010 --> 00:00:42,070
Disintegrated in order to create a new sheldon.

14
00:00:43,080 --> 00:00:44,590
Would the new sheldon be in any way

15
00:00:44,600 --> 00:00:47,070
An improvement on the old sheldon?

16
00:00:48,130 --> 00:00:50,470
No, he would be exactly the same.

17
00:00:50,580 --> 00:00:52,300
That is a problem.

18
00:00:52,720 --> 00:00:54,510
So you see it too.

19
00:00:54,780 --> 00:00:55,790
Dr. hofstadter.

20
00:00:55,800 --> 00:00:56,980
Dr. cooper.

21
00:00:56,990 --> 00:00:58,550
Dr. gablehauser. dr. gablehauser.

22
00:00:58,560 --> 00:01:01,010
Gentlemen, I'd like you to meet dennis kim.

23
00:01:01,020 --> 00:01:04,300
Dennis is a highly sought-after doctoral candidate,

24
00:01:04,300 --> 00:01:06,190
And we're hopingto have him do his graduate work here.

25
00:01:06,200 --> 00:01:07,720
Graduate work. very impressive.

26
00:01:07,730 --> 00:01:09,770
And he's only 15 years old.

27
00:01:09,780 --> 00:01:13,630
Not bad-- i, myself, started graduate school at 14.

28
00:01:13,640 --> 00:01:15,530
Well, I lost a year while my family

29
00:01:15,540 --> 00:01:17,880
Was tunneling out of north korea.

30
00:01:19,730 --> 00:01:22,720
Advantage Kim.

31
00:01:23,020 --> 00:01:24,070
I thought maybe you boys

32
00:01:24,080 --> 00:01:25,330
Could show dennis around.

33
00:01:25,340 --> 00:01:26,360
Let him see why we're

34
00:01:26,370 --> 00:01:28,800
The best physics research facility in the country.

35
00:01:28,810 --> 00:01:30,070
I already know you're not.

36
00:01:30,080 --> 00:01:31,920
You don't have an open science grid computer

37
00:01:31,930 --> 00:01:34,660
Or a free electron laser, and the string theory research

38
00:01:34,670 --> 00:01:36,390
Being done here is nothing but a dead end.

39
00:01:36,400 --> 00:01:38,130
Excuse me, that is my research,

40
00:01:38,140 --> 00:01:40,050
And it is by no means a dead end.

41
00:01:40,060 --> 00:01:41,920
Well, obviously you don't see it yet,

42
00:01:41,930 --> 00:01:43,900
But trust me, you will.

43
00:01:45,960 --> 00:01:47,660
Dennis, we've discussed this.

44
00:01:47,670 --> 00:01:50,230
We're in the process of updating our equipment,

45
00:01:50,240 --> 00:01:52,410
And we welcome your input on our research goals,

46
00:01:52,420 --> 00:01:54,340
And we've agreed to look the other way if you want

47
00:01:54,350 --> 00:01:56,360
To use up to 20% of the grant money you attract

48
00:01:56,370 --> 00:01:59,030
To smuggle your grandfather out of pyongyang.

49
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:02,600
We want him here, boys.

50
00:02:02,610 --> 00:02:03,770
Make it happen.

51
00:02:03,780 --> 00:02:04,520
Yes, sir.

52
00:02:04,530 --> 00:02:05,640
You can count on us.

53
00:02:05,650 --> 00:02:06,450
We're on it.

54
00:02:06,460 --> 00:02:08,750
What the hell do you mean, "dead end"?

55
00:02:09,030 --> 00:02:11,460
I mean, the whole landscape of false vacuums

56
00:02:11,470 --> 00:02:13,030
In string theory could be as large as ten

57
00:02:13,040 --> 00:02:14,550
To the five hundredth power.

58
00:02:14,560 --> 00:02:17,950
In addition-- ooh, look, chocolate milk.

59
00:02:20,200 --> 00:02:22,600
I sense a disturbance in the force.

60
00:02:23,360 --> 00:02:25,240
A bad feeling I have about this.

61
00:02:45,560 --> 00:02:48,560
The.Jerusalem.Duality

62
00:02:52,010 --> 00:02:53,800
So, dennis, how long have you been in america?

63
00:02:53,810 --> 00:02:54,870
A year and a half.

64
00:02:54,880 --> 00:02:55,600
No kidding.

65
00:02:55,610 --> 00:02:56,720
You speak english really well.

66
00:02:56,730 --> 00:02:58,350
So do you.

67
00:02:59,210 --> 00:03:00,580
Except for your tendency to end

68
00:03:00,590 --> 00:03:03,060
Sentences with prepositions.

69
00:03:03,840 --> 00:03:05,620
What are you talking about?

70
00:03:07,290 --> 00:03:08,790
That.

71
00:03:09,530 --> 00:03:11,310
He's not wrong.

72
00:03:11,870 --> 00:03:12,890
All right...

73
00:03:12,900 --> 00:03:14,180
And this is my office.

74
00:03:14,190 --> 00:03:15,140
Is this part of the tour?

75
00:03:15,150 --> 00:03:17,010
Nope. good-bye.

76
00:03:17,850 --> 00:03:19,910
Come on, sheldon, we've hardly shown him anything.

77
00:03:19,920 --> 00:03:21,420
All right.

78
00:03:21,430 --> 00:03:22,850
This is my desk.

79
00:03:22,860 --> 00:03:24,930
These are my books; this is my door.

80
00:03:24,940 --> 00:03:27,450
Please close it behind you. good-bye.

81
00:03:28,790 --> 00:03:30,010
Looks like you're doing work

82
00:03:30,020 --> 00:03:31,340
In quantum loop corrections.

83
00:03:31,350 --> 00:03:32,370
Keen observation.

84
00:03:32,380 --> 00:03:34,150
Good-bye.

85
00:03:34,180 --> 00:03:36,600
You see where you went wrong, don't you?

86
00:03:36,830 --> 00:03:38,360
Leonard?

87
00:03:38,370 --> 00:03:39,970
Yeah?

88
00:03:40,160 --> 00:03:41,940
Get him out.

89
00:03:41,950 --> 00:03:42,740
Come on, dennis.

90
00:03:42,750 --> 00:03:43,940
I'll show you the recreation center.

91
00:03:43,950 --> 00:03:45,290
They've got nautilus equipment.

92
00:03:45,300 --> 00:03:47,740
Do I look like I lift weights?

93
00:03:48,200 --> 00:03:50,270
Not heavy ones.

94
00:03:50,740 --> 00:03:52,430
It's startling to me you haven't considered

95
00:03:52,440 --> 00:03:55,490
A lorentz invariant or field theory approach.

96
00:03:55,540 --> 00:03:57,240
You think I haven't considered it?

97
00:03:57,250 --> 00:03:59,420
You really think I haven't considered it?

98
00:03:59,430 --> 00:04:01,280
Have you considered it?

99
00:04:02,180 --> 00:04:03,250
Get him out, leonard.

100
00:04:03,260 --> 00:04:04,660
Come on, dennis.

101
00:04:04,670 --> 00:04:06,870
I'll show you the radiation lab.

102
00:04:07,650 --> 00:04:10,100
Wow, you won the stevenson award?

103
00:04:10,110 --> 00:04:11,030
Yes.

104
00:04:11,040 --> 00:04:14,460
In fact, I am the youngest person ever to win it.

105
00:04:14,470 --> 00:04:16,610
Really? how old?

106
00:04:16,620 --> 00:04:18,570
Fourteen and a half.

107
00:04:18,580 --> 00:04:21,480
Hm, you were the youngest person ever to win it.

108
00:04:24,270 --> 00:04:25,500
It's like looking into an obnoxious

109
00:04:25,510 --> 00:04:28,220
Little mirror, isn't it?

110
00:04:31,840 --> 00:04:34,380
This is really delicious, isn't it?

111
00:04:37,700 --> 00:04:40,770
Still can't talk to me unless you're drunk, huh?

112
00:04:41,990 --> 00:04:46,050
Oh, sweetie, you are so damaged.

113
00:04:47,090 --> 00:04:49,500
Hey, I'm damaged, too.

114
00:04:49,950 --> 00:04:52,090
How about a hug for howie?

115
00:04:52,660 --> 00:04:55,290
Sure. raj, hug howard.

116
00:04:59,840 --> 00:05:02,030
Something you'd like to share?

117
00:05:02,470 --> 00:05:05,220
A tale of woe, perhaps?

118
00:05:05,280 --> 00:05:06,980
Fifteen years old.

119
00:05:06,990 --> 00:05:10,990
Dennis Kim's 15 years old, and he's already correcting my work.

120
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:14,810
Today, I went from being wolfgang amadeus mozart, to...

121
00:05:14,820 --> 00:05:16,210
You know, that other guy.

122
00:05:16,220 --> 00:05:18,220
Antonio salieri?

123
00:05:18,470 --> 00:05:21,940
Oh god, now even you're smarter than me.

124
00:05:22,330 --> 00:05:23,350
You know, sheldon, you don't have

125
00:05:23,360 --> 00:05:24,880
So many friends that you can afford

126
00:05:24,890 --> 00:05:27,020
To start insulting them.

127
00:05:27,770 --> 00:05:29,710
Just eat, sheldon, you'll feel better.

128
00:05:29,720 --> 00:05:31,230
Why waste food?

129
00:05:31,240 --> 00:05:32,760
In texas, when a cow goes dry,

130
00:05:32,770 --> 00:05:34,860
They don't keep feeding it, they just take her out

131
00:05:34,870 --> 00:05:37,020
And shoot her between the eyes.

132
00:05:37,580 --> 00:05:38,580
I'm confused.

133
00:05:38,590 --> 00:05:40,950
Did sheldon stop giving milk?

134
00:05:42,090 --> 00:05:43,990
You can't let this kid get to you.

135
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:46,180
You always knew that someday someone would come along

136
00:05:46,190 --> 00:05:47,510
Who was younger and smarter.

137
00:05:47,520 --> 00:05:49,420
Yes, but I assumed I would've been dead

138
00:05:49,430 --> 00:05:51,080
Hundreds of years and that there'd be

139
00:05:51,090 --> 00:05:54,370
An asterisk by his name because he'd be a cyborg.

140
00:05:55,340 --> 00:05:56,860
So you've got a little competition.

141
00:05:56,870 --> 00:05:58,700
I really don't see what the big deal is.

142
00:05:58,710 --> 00:05:59,850
Well, of course you don't.

143
00:05:59,860 --> 00:06:02,450
You've never excelled at anything.

144
00:06:03,960 --> 00:06:05,510
I don't understand-- exactly how

145
00:06:05,520 --> 00:06:07,840
Did he get any friends in the first place?

146
00:06:08,560 --> 00:06:10,740
We liked leonard.

147
00:06:12,550 --> 00:06:14,710
Well, what are you gonna do, sheldon, just give up?

148
00:06:14,720 --> 00:06:16,060
Yes.

149
00:06:16,070 --> 00:06:17,480
It's what a rational person does

150
00:06:17,490 --> 00:06:19,560
When his entire life's work is invalidated

151
00:06:19,570 --> 00:06:22,340
By a postpubescent asian wunderkind.

152
00:06:23,230 --> 00:06:24,990
He ceases his fruitless efforts,

153
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:27,830
He donates his body to scientific research,

154
00:06:27,840 --> 00:06:29,800
And he waits to die.

155
00:06:31,280 --> 00:06:32,380
You know, I'm confused again.

156
00:06:32,390 --> 00:06:33,910
Is he waiting, or do we get

157
00:06:33,920 --> 00:06:36,140
To shoot him between the eyes?

158
00:06:40,980 --> 00:06:42,300
I've decided you're right.

159
00:06:42,310 --> 00:06:44,100
My career is not over.

160
00:06:44,110 --> 00:06:44,980
Great!

161
00:06:44,990 --> 00:06:46,530
But since the arrival of dennis kim

162
00:06:46,540 --> 00:06:48,160
Has rendered my research pointless,

163
00:06:48,170 --> 00:06:50,740
I just have to find something else to focus on.

164
00:06:50,750 --> 00:06:51,690
Great!

165
00:06:51,700 --> 00:06:55,140
So I've decided I'm going to collaborate with you.

166
00:06:57,840 --> 00:06:59,480
Great.

167
00:06:59,490 --> 00:07:02,210
So what exactly is it you do?

168
00:07:02,500 --> 00:07:04,370
I know you chatter on about it all the time,

169
00:07:04,380 --> 00:07:06,600
But I've never really paid attention.

170
00:07:07,220 --> 00:07:09,400
Okay, well, right now I'm designing an experiment

171
00:07:09,410 --> 00:07:11,410
To study the soft component of cosmic radiation

172
00:07:11,420 --> 00:07:13,360
At sea level, but I really don't need any help.

173
00:07:13,370 --> 00:07:15,230
Oh, sure you do.

174
00:07:15,950 --> 00:07:17,610
Now, see, what's this here in the schematic?

175
00:07:17,620 --> 00:07:18,890
Is that a laser array?

176
00:07:18,900 --> 00:07:21,120
Yes. now, hm...

177
00:07:23,770 --> 00:07:25,640
What happens if you use argon lasers

178
00:07:25,650 --> 00:07:27,320
Instead of helium-neon?

179
00:07:27,330 --> 00:07:28,800
It would blow up.

180
00:07:28,810 --> 00:07:31,070
Are you sure? pretty sure.

181
00:07:31,080 --> 00:07:32,730
"pretty sure" is not very scientific.

182
00:07:32,740 --> 00:07:34,180
Is this how you normally work?

183
00:07:34,190 --> 00:07:36,880
Just hunches and guesses and stuff?

184
00:07:37,320 --> 00:07:40,250
Okay, sheldon, I understand that you're

185
00:07:40,260 --> 00:07:41,920
Going through a bit of a career crisis,

186
00:07:41,930 --> 00:07:43,630
You're searching for some other area

187
00:07:43,640 --> 00:07:45,860
Where you can feel valuable and productive,

188
00:07:45,870 --> 00:07:47,060
But I need to tell you something,

189
00:07:47,070 --> 00:07:48,520
And I want you to listen carefully.

190
00:07:48,530 --> 00:07:49,420
All right.

191
00:07:49,430 --> 00:07:51,570
Go away.

192
00:07:54,140 --> 00:07:55,770
If you're concerned about sharing credit with me,

193
00:07:55,780 --> 00:07:58,140
Your name can go first-- I'm going.

194
00:08:02,770 --> 00:08:05,440
It's a small, brown paper bag, ma!

195
00:08:05,450 --> 00:08:07,530
I'm looking in it right now.

196
00:08:08,510 --> 00:08:09,820
Why would I make that up?

197
00:08:09,830 --> 00:08:12,670
There's no ding dong in it.

198
00:08:15,020 --> 00:08:18,990
How are two ding dongs tomorrow gonna help me today?

199
00:08:20,570 --> 00:08:21,990
So...

200
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:23,960
This is engineering, huh?

201
00:08:23,970 --> 00:08:25,770
I'll talk to you later.

202
00:08:25,780 --> 00:08:27,270
Engineering.

203
00:08:27,280 --> 00:08:29,340
Where the noble semiskilled laborers

204
00:08:29,350 --> 00:08:32,250
Execute the vision of those who think and dream.

205
00:08:33,120 --> 00:08:35,570
Hello, oompah loompahs of science.

206
00:08:37,660 --> 00:08:40,070
Sheldon, what are you doing here?

207
00:08:40,080 --> 00:08:41,880
I just came by to say hello.

208
00:08:41,890 --> 00:08:43,510
I've been in this lab for three years.

209
00:08:43,520 --> 00:08:44,970
You've never come by to say hello.

210
00:08:44,980 --> 00:08:48,050
Well, up until now, I've had better things to do.

211
00:08:48,610 --> 00:08:50,760
So, what are we making today?

212
00:08:50,770 --> 00:08:53,110
A small payload support structure

213
00:08:53,120 --> 00:08:55,130
For a european science experimental package

214
00:08:55,140 --> 00:08:56,800
That's going up on the next space shuttle.

215
00:08:56,810 --> 00:08:58,800
Really? how does it work?

216
00:08:58,810 --> 00:09:00,370
When this is done, it will be attached

217
00:09:00,380 --> 00:09:02,470
To the payload bay, and the sensor apparatus

218
00:09:02,480 --> 00:09:04,380
Will rest on it.

219
00:09:06,800 --> 00:09:08,880
So it's a shelf.

220
00:09:12,320 --> 00:09:14,730
No, you don't understand-- during acceleration,

221
00:09:14,740 --> 00:09:17,050
It needs to stay perfectly level and provide...

222
00:09:17,060 --> 00:09:19,270
Yeah, okay, it's a shelf.

223
00:09:19,330 --> 00:09:20,990
Now, I notice you're using titanium.

224
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:23,770
Did you give any consideration to carbon nanotubes?

225
00:09:23,780 --> 00:09:26,530
They're lighter, cheaper, and have twice the tensile strength.

226
00:09:26,540 --> 00:09:27,750
Sheldon,

227
00:09:27,760 --> 00:09:29,470
There's a diploma in my office

228
00:09:29,480 --> 00:09:32,340
That says I have a master's in engineering.

229
00:09:32,350 --> 00:09:33,620
And you also have a note from your mother

230
00:09:33,630 --> 00:09:35,970
That says, "i love you, bubula."

231
00:09:38,130 --> 00:09:39,810
But neither of those is a cogent argument

232
00:09:39,820 --> 00:09:42,060
For titanium over nanotubes.

233
00:09:42,960 --> 00:09:43,850
Sheldon? yes?

234
00:09:43,860 --> 00:09:46,140
Go away!

235
00:09:46,840 --> 00:09:48,950
Did leonard tell you to say that?

236
00:09:48,960 --> 00:09:51,410
No, I thought of it all by myself.

237
00:09:52,320 --> 00:09:55,970
That can't be a coincidence.

238
00:09:55,980 --> 00:09:59,130
There must be some causal link I'm missing.

239
00:10:01,670 --> 00:10:04,290
Go away!

240
00:10:12,060 --> 00:10:14,740
Curiouser and curiouser.

241
00:10:18,950 --> 00:10:19,940
Is he here?

242
00:10:19,950 --> 00:10:22,080
If he were, I wouldn't be.

243
00:10:22,870 --> 00:10:24,010
Do you know what he did?

244
00:10:24,020 --> 00:10:25,380
He watched me work for ten minutes,

245
00:10:25,390 --> 00:10:26,860
And then started to design a simple piece

246
00:10:26,870 --> 00:10:29,200
Of software that could replace me.

247
00:10:30,720 --> 00:10:32,300
Is that even possible?

248
00:10:32,310 --> 00:10:34,350
As it turns out, yes.

249
00:10:35,960 --> 00:10:37,870
Something's got to be done about him, leonard.

250
00:10:37,880 --> 00:10:38,890
Like what?

251
00:10:38,900 --> 00:10:40,380
He'll never be able to cope with the fact

252
00:10:40,390 --> 00:10:42,120
That some 15-year-old kid is smarter

253
00:10:42,130 --> 00:10:44,580
And more accomplished than he is.

254
00:10:44,590 --> 00:10:46,220
Well, what if something were to happen to this boy

255
00:10:46,230 --> 00:10:48,750
So he was no longer a threat to sheldon?

256
00:10:50,150 --> 00:10:52,470
Then our problem would be solved.

257
00:10:52,970 --> 00:10:56,810
Hang on, are we talking about murdering dennis kim?

258
00:10:57,310 --> 00:10:59,340
I'm not saying no.

259
00:11:00,080 --> 00:11:01,440
We don't have to go that far.

260
00:11:01,450 --> 00:11:03,030
There are other means available.

261
00:11:03,040 --> 00:11:05,510
We can't send him back to north korea.

262
00:11:05,720 --> 00:11:07,630
He knows how to get out.

263
00:11:09,570 --> 00:11:10,620
The only thing we need to do

264
00:11:10,630 --> 00:11:12,570
Is make this kim kid lose his focus.

265
00:11:12,580 --> 00:11:13,600
Won't happen.

266
00:11:13,610 --> 00:11:15,860
He is not interested in anything but physics.

267
00:11:15,870 --> 00:11:17,120
What about biology?

268
00:11:17,130 --> 00:11:20,580
What? you know, biology.

269
00:11:20,910 --> 00:11:22,960
The one thing that can completely derail

270
00:11:22,970 --> 00:11:24,170
A world-class mind.

271
00:11:24,180 --> 00:11:25,610
Howard, he's 15.

272
00:11:25,620 --> 00:11:26,420
Yeah, so?

